|~ `::quote`, the kind of code held as a value, and what is declared
   under it: its constructor and its refusals, the refusal of text read
   as code and the facts it carries, and the words that read a quote. A
   quote is also the vector of its steps, so the words of vectors read
   it as one, and a quote under a tag, a link or a snippet of a page,
   reads them through its shape.
   The decisions it exists for: [D114]. ~|

:quote
  |~~ A quote is code held as a value, `~(add 1 | mul 2)`: steps that
      run only when something runs them. A word that runs code later
      takes a quote, `map ~(mul 2)`, `filter ~(gt 1)`, `sort /name`,
      and `apply` runs one on a subject, `5 | apply ~(add 1)` answering
      `6`.

      It is written `~(…)` around a pipeline, and one name drops the
      parentheses, `~count`. A word that runs code takes a path bare,
      `sort /name`, so a quote of a path alone is refused where it is
      written; one built, by `parse` or by `tag ::quote`, answers the
      path and prints under its tag, `::quote[/name]`. A
      quote is the vector of its steps under `::quote`,
      `~(add 1 | mul 2) | count` answering `2`, and each step reads as
      data: a literal as itself, and a step
      that computes as a record, `~(add 1) | first` answering
      `::call{:name :add :modifiers [1]}`. `parse` turns text into a
      quote and a quote into its text, and JSON writes a quote as its
      text.

      Because code is a value, a page holds links and snippets that run,
      the laws of a name are quotes that answer true, and the trail of a
      failure holds the steps it skipped, ready to replay.
      ::link~(::call | doc) ::link~(::any/apply | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/code | doc)
  ~~|

:quote/countsItsSteps
  |~~ A quote of three steps counts three. ~~|
  ::law~(~(1 | add 1 | mul 2) | count | eq 3)

:quote/printsADeclarationALine
  |~~ A quote of declarations alone prints a declaration a line. ~~|
  ::law~(~(:a 1 | :b 2) | parse | eq ":a 1
| :b 2")

:quote/equalsItsCopy
  |~~ Two quotes of one code are equal. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1) | eq ~(add 1))

:quote/printsAPathAloneUnderItsTag
  |~~ A quote of a path alone, built, prints under its tag. ~~|
  ::law~("/name" | parse | print | eq "::quote[/name]")

:quote/answersThePathItHoldsAlone
  |~~ A quote of a path alone answers the path, and reads nothing. ~~|
  ::law~({:name 1} | apply ("/name" | parse) | eq /name)

:quote/buildsBackFromItsSteps
  |~~ The steps of a quote build it back. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | payload | tag ::quote | eq ~(add 1 | mul 2))

:quote/filtersItsSteps
  |~~ A quote filters as the vector of its steps. ~~|
  ::law~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | filter ~(get /name | eq :mul) | parse | eq "mul 2")

:quote/construct
  |~~ The quote of a vector whose every element is a step. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:quote/PayloadNotVecError
  |~~ `::quote` builds code from the vector of its steps; a payload of
      another kind is refused.
      ::link~(::quote | doc)
  ~~|

:quote/PayloadNotVecError/refusesAMap
  |~~ A map payload is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(::quote{:not :steps} !| [type (get /actualType)] | eq [::quote/PayloadNotVecError ::map])

:quote/ElementNotStepError
  |~~ Every element of a quote is a step: a literal, a path, a quote, a
      vector, a set or a map of steps, or a step as data, `::call`,
      `::bind` and their kin. A value with no reading as code, a value
      under a tag of another kind among them, is refused at the index it
      stands at.
      ::link~(::quote | doc)
  ~~|

:quote/ElementNotStepError/refusesAValueWithNoReading
  |~~ A value with no reading as a step is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([::Box[1]] | tag ::quote !| [type (get /index)] | eq [::quote/ElementNotStepError 0])

:quote/ReadBackDiffersError
  |~~ The steps print as text that reads as no steps, and `:printed` is
      that text: a value assembled after the first step prints as a value
      written as a later step, which no pipeline holds. A value a step answers is built by a word,
      `get [1 2]`, and a value under a tag by `tag`.
      ::link~(::quote | doc) ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)
  ~~|

:quote/ReadBackDiffersError/refusesAValueAfterTheHead
  |~~ A value after the first step is refused, its text naming it. ~~|
  ::law~([[1] [2]] | tag ::quote !| [type (get /printed)] | eq [::quote/ReadBackDiffersError "[1] | [2]"])

:quote/ReadBackDiffersError/keepsAValueAtTheHead
  |~~ A value at the head is the subject the steps after it run on. ~~|
  ::law~([[1 2] (~(count) | first)] | tag ::quote | apply it | eq 2)

:quote/ParseError
  |~~ The source does not read as qlang: `:marker` points where the
      reading stopped, `:expected` lists what could stand there, and a
      source cut short names the bracket it left open.
      ::link~(::quote | doc)
  ~~|

:quote/ParseError/source
  |~~ The line of the text where the reading stopped. ~~|
  ::string

:quote/ParseError/marker
  |~~ A caret under the place it stopped. ~~|
  ::string

:quote/ParseError/message
  |~~ What the grammar says of the place, a bracket left open among it. ~~|
  ::string

:quote/ParseError/expected
  |~~ What the reading could take there. ~~|
  ::vec

:quote/ParseError/found
  |~~ What it found there. ~~|
  ::string

:quote/ParseError/location
  |~~ The span of the text where the reading stopped. ~~|
  ::span

:quote/ParseError/uri
  |~~ The source the text came from. ~~|
  ::string

:quote/ParseError/refusesASourceCutShort
  |~~ A source cut short after a pipe is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("42 |" | parse !| type | eq ::quote/ParseError)

:quote/parse
  |~~ Answers the source of the quote, a string, `~(add 1 | mul 2) |
      parse` answering `"add 1 | mul 2"`: the reverse of `parse` on a
      string, which reads text as a quote, so the source parsed again is
      the quote, `~(add 1) | parse | parse` answering `~(add 1)`. The
      source is printed in one spelling, so the spaces and the line
      breaks the quote was written with are not kept, `~(add   1) |
      parse` answering `"add 1"`. A link or a snippet out of a page
      answers the query it holds, without its tags, and the empty quote
      the empty string.
      ::link~(::string/parse | doc) ::link~(::quote/open | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The text the quote reads back from. ~~| ::string
    | law :answersTheSource
        |~~ A quote parses to its source. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | parse | eq "add 1")
    | law :readsBackAsTheQuote
        |~~ The source parsed again is the quote. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | :q it | q | parse | parse | eq q)
    | law :printsOneSpelling
        |~~ The spaces the quote was written with are not kept. ~~|
        ~(~(add   1 |   mul 2) | parse | eq "add 1 | mul 2")
    | law :leavesTheTagsOfALink
        |~~ A link answers the query it holds, without its tags. ~~|
        ~(::link~(1 | add 1) | parse | eq "1 | add 1")
    | law :answersNothingForTheEmptyQuote
        |~~ The empty quote parses to the empty string. ~~|
        ~(~() | parse | eq "")
    | impl ::builtin{}

:quote/open
  |~~ Answers what the query the quote holds answers, run beneath
      whatever tags stand over it, `~(1 | add 1) | open` answering `2`:
      a link opens to the page it names, so `links | map ~open` reads every
      page a page names in one query, and a snippet to the answer it
      shows. The query runs in the scope of the reader, so a name bound
      before it is read, `:k 5 | ~(k) | open` answering `5`, and the
      names it binds stay inside it. It runs from null, not from the
      subject of `open`: a query that reads a subject, `~(add 1)`, is
      refused on null, and a quote meant for a subject runs against it
      by `apply`, `5 | apply ~(add 1)`. Text is read as a quote first by
      `parse`, `"1 | add 1" | parse | open`.
      ::link~(::any/apply | doc) ::link~(::doc/links | doc)
      ::link~(::doc/snippets | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ What the query answers, a value of any kind. ~~| ::any
    | throws :SubjectNotQuoteError
        |~~ `open` runs a quote, `~(1 | add 1) | open`: text is read as a
            quote first by `parse`, `"1 | add 1" | parse | open`, and the
            links of a page are read by `links`, `::vec | doc | links |
            map ~open`.
        ~~|
    | law :runsALink
        |~~ A link opens to the answer of its query. ~~|
        ~(|~~ ::link~(1 | add 1) ~~| | links | first | open | eq 2)
    | law :runsASnippet
        |~~ A snippet opens to what it shows. ~~|
        ~(|~~ ::snippet~([3 1 2] | sort) ~~| | snippets | first | open | eq [1 2 3])
    | law :readsTheScopeOfTheReader
        |~~ A name bound before the quote is read inside it. ~~|
        ~(:k 5 | ~(k) | open | eq 5)
    | law :keepsItsNamesInside
        |~~ A name the query binds stays inside it. ~~|
        ~(:k 5 | ~(:k 1) | open | k | eq 5)
    | law :runsFromNull
        |~~ The query runs from null. ~~| ~(~(type) | open | eq ::null)
    | law :runsTextReadByParse
        |~~ Text read as a quote by `parse` opens. ~~|
        ~("1 | add 1" | parse | open | eq 2)
    | law :SubjectNotQuoteError/refusesAString
        |~~ Text is no quote until `parse` reads it. ~~|
        ~("1 | add 1" | open !| [type (get /actualType)] | eq [::quote/open/SubjectNotQuoteError ::string])
    | law :refusesAQueryThatReadsASubject
        |~~ A query that reads a subject is refused on null. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | open !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

